# Games, each played far enough in to show a board, a hand or a room
# rather than a title screen.  One stanza per program; every stanza makes
# whatever it needs for itself.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- adventure

shot    advcom
size    92 80
cap     The ADVSYS compiler turning the sample adventure into the world file
cap     the interpreter plays: it names every function, object and method as
cap     it reads them, and ends by counting the words, objects and code.  The
cap     sample is in GAMES/ADVSYS; run it in a directory holding osample.adv
cap     and objects.adi.
try     advcom osample
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample.adv > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/osample.adv
wait    4
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects.adi > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/objects.adi
wait    4
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/GAMESA
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     advcom osample
wait    45

shot    advint
size    24 80
cap     The ADVSYS interpreter, playing the world advcom has just built from
cap     the sample source: you start in the livingroom, `n' takes you to the
cap     hallway and `e' into the storage room, where there is a rusty key.
cap     Type at the `:' prompt.  Run it in the directory holding the world
cap     file.
try     advint osample
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample.adv > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/osample.adv
wait    4
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects.adi > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/objects.adi
wait    4
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/GAMESA
wait    1
run     advcom osample > /nil
wait    45
run     clear
wait    1
run     advint osample
wait    12
send    look\r
wait    5
send    n\r
wait    5
send    e\r
wait    6

shot    advent
size    48 80
cap     Colossal Cave: it loads its twelve sections of text, offers
cap     instructions, and drops you on the road outside the brick building.
cap     `in' takes you into the well house, where the lamp and the keys are;
cap     `xyzzy' is the magic word.  Its data is in GAMES/adv.
try     advent
run     advent
wait    8
send    no\r
wait    5
send    in\r
wait    5
send    take lamp\r
wait    5
send    take keys\r
wait    5
send    xyzzy\r
wait    6

shot    infocom.tcap
cap     An interpreter for Infocom's Z-machine, playing dejavu.z3: the status
cap     line across the top, in reverse video (each # a lit blank), names the
cap     room and the score, with the story below it and a `>' prompt for
cap     commands like `look' and `inventory'.  Three Inform demonstration
cap     stories ship in GAMES/INFORM -- dejavu, hellow and shell.
try     infocom.tcap dejavu.z3
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES/INFORM
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     infocom.tcap dejavu.z3
wait    8
send    \r
wait    4
send    look\r
wait    5
send    \r
wait    4
send    inventory\r
wait    6

shot    inform
cap     inform compiling hellow.inf, the source of one of the three demos in
cap     GAMES/INFORM, into hellow.z3, a story file that infocom plays; ls -l
cap     shows the file it wrote.  Name the source without its .inf; the
cap     library headers it includes are in SRC/inform beside it.
try     inform hellow
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/INFK > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/INFK
run     cp /dd/SRC/inform/hellow.inf /dd/SRC/inform/parser.h /dd/SRC/inform/verblib.h /dd/SRC/inform/grammar.h /dd/tmp/INFK
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/INFK
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     inform hellow
wait    15
run     ls -l hellow.z3
wait    2

shot    infocom
cap     A Z-machine interpreter playing dejavu.z3 from GAMES/INFORM. It writes
cap     the cursor codes for its status line as literal text, which is the row
cap     of brackets down the left, and still plays the story; infocom.tcap is
cap     the build for a terminal.  RETURN clears each `More' prompt.
try     infocom dejavu.z3
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES/INFORM
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     infocom dejavu.z3
wait    8
send    \r
wait    5
send    \r
wait    5
send    look\r
wait    6

shot    paranoia
size    36 80
pages
cap     The Paranoia text adventure.  It rolls you a Troubleshooter --
cap     attributes, secret society, mutant power, equipment -- and then
cap     wakes you on the floor of a briefing room.  RETURN clears each
cap     `More' and goes on; you get six clones.
try     paranoia
run     clear
wait    1
run     paranoia
wait    8
snap
send    \r
wait    4
send    \r
wait    4
send    \r
wait    5
snap

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- arcade

shot    lander
cap     A lunar lander flown over a scrolling landscape.  Space starts a
cap     descent, a digit sets the engine power, `x' or `k' fires the main
cap     thruster and `z'/`j' and `c'/`l' the side retros; the numbers on the
cap     pads are what each is worth.  The top line is altitude, position and
cap     the two rates of drift, the bottom line thrust and fuel.  Its score
cap     table is GAMES/lander.hs.
try     lander
run     lander
wait    8
send    \s
wait    4
send    5
wait    3
send    x
wait    4
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    mz
size    24 80
cap     Skill 3: the p is you, each c a monster taking the shortest way to
cap     you, and the dots are the pellets you eat for points.
try     mz
run     clear
run     mz
wait    4
send    n\r
wait    3
send    3\r
wait    4
keys    666
wait    2
keys    222
wait    3
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    pacman
size    26 80
cap     Pac-Man in an ASCII maze, with your system's tmode loaded, which it
cap     forks to set the terminal.  It asks your name and whether you want
cap     instructions, then draws the maze by moving the cursor with single
cap     control characters that an ANSI terminal like this one does not
cap     follow, so only the maze's top wall reaches the screen: the board does
cap     not draw here.  Its maze, help and score files are in GAMES/pacman.
try     pacman
run     load /h1/CMDS/tmode
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     pacman
send    Tester\r
wait    3
send    y
wait    4
snap
send    \s
wait    2
send    q
wait    2

shot    robots
size    24 80
cap     Robots: you are the `I', the robots are the `#', and they walk
cap     straight at you every time you move -- steer them into each other and
cap     they become junk heaps, `@'.  The panel on the right keeps the keys,
cap     the screen, robot and score counts.  `robots -s' prints the top ten on
cap     its own.
try     robots -m
run     robots -m
wait    8
keys    5
wait    3
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    snake
cap     Snake: you are the `I', the money is the `$' and the snake is the
cap     line of `s' with `S' for its head.  Move with h, j, k and l and
cap     reach the money before the snake reaches you; `x' quits.
try     snake
run     snake
wait    10
snap
send    x
wait    3

shot    worm
size    24 80
cap     The worm heading right along the middle of the field, the `@' its
cap     head, and a 6 below it to eat.  Control-C leaves.
try     worm
run     worm
wait    3
send    k
wait    2
send    l
wait    3
send    j
wait    2
send    L
wait    3
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    sokoban
cap     Level 1, drawn two characters to a square: six packets, the `$$', the
cap     storage squares, `....', at the right, and you, the `@@'.  The status
cap     line counts the packets, the ones stored and your moves and pushes.
try     sokoban
run     sokoban
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    tet
cap     A game under way: pieces stacking in the well on the left, the next
cap     piece in the box at the top right and the keys in the menu beside it.
cap     `q' leaves to the high-score table in GAMES/tet.hs.  It reads its keys
cap     from a terminal.
try     tet
run     tet
wait    8
keys    p
wait    5
send    \s
wait    3
send    \s
wait    3
send    \s
wait    4
snap
send    q
wait    3

shot    wanderer
cap     Wanderer, a Boulderdash: `<|>' is you, the dots are earth you can
cap     dig through and the diamonds are what you are after -- forty-five on
cap     the first screen, with a thousand moves to get them.  Its 49
cap     screens are in GAMES/WAND.
try     wanderer
run     wanderer
wait    10
snap
send    q
wait    3

# -------------------------------------------------------------- board & card

shot    back
size    40 80
burst
cap     The opening position, one side's checkers drawn as [] and the other's
cap     as <>, with the empty dice cups, the command menu and the doubling
cap     status on the right, waiting at `Command?'.
try     back
run     export TERM=vt100
run     clear
run     back

shot    blackjack
size    24 80
cap     Las Vegas blackjack in BASIC09, one hand in: it
cap     asks your name and whether you want the rules, then takes a wager
cap     and deals.  RETURN draws a card, `s' stands, `d' doubles down, `x'
cap     splits a pair; a wager of 0 ends the game.  It runs through
cap     Microware's runb -- `blackjack' at your own OS-9 shell, `runb
cap     blackjack' here.
try     runb blackjack
run     load /h1/CMDS/runb
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     runb blackjack
wait    4
send    Tester\r
wait    3
send    n\r
wait    3
send    25\r
wait    4
send    s\r
wait    6

shot    blackjak
cap     Las Vegas blackjack, with a hand dealt and a bet on the table: it
cap     gives you a stake of chips, asks what you want to bet, deals two cards
cap     against the dealer's one, and then asks whether to hit or stick.
cap     It wants the word `no', not `N'.  One of four games written in
cap     SNOBOL4; their data is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     blackjak
run     blackjak
wait    6
send    no\r
wait    5
send    10\r
wait    8

shot    chess
cap     One move in, playing white: your pieces carry a star, e2-e4 has been
cap     played, and the machine's reply, e7-e5, is on the line under the board
cap     with the prompt for your next move.
try     chess
run     chess
wait    8
keys    w
wait    4
send    Tester\r
wait    4
keys    s
wait    6
keys    e2
wait    5
keys    e4
wait    25
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    crib
cap     Cribbage: your hand fanned out on the left, the two pegging tracks
cap     on the right, and the cut that decides whose crib it is.  Discard by
cap     naming a card -- `7H' for the seven of hearts.  It needs TERM set,
cap     so run it from a login session.  Its instructions are in
cap     GAMES/CRIB.
try     crib
run     crib
wait    8
send    n\r
wait    4
send    S\r
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    cribbage
cap     Another build of the BSD cribbage that crib is: your hand fanned out
cap     on the left, the pegging board on the right, and the cut for the crib
cap     under it, waiting for a discard.  It needs TERM set.
try     cribbage
run     cribbage
wait    8
send    n\r
wait    4
send    s\r
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    gnuan
size    50 80
cap     GNU Chess's analyser annotating a five-move game from a file of
cap     moves, one move pair to a line: the opening moves it finds in the
cap     book are marked `book', each later one is scored with the best line
cap     it searched, and the closing percentages say how often each side
cap     played the move it would have chosen.  A line reading `draw' ends
cap     the game.
try     gnuan
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G4GNUAN > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G4GNUAN
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G4GNUAN
run     echo "e2e4 e7e5" > moves; echo "g1f3 b8c6" >> moves; echo "f1b5 a7a6" >> moves
run     echo "h2h3 h7h6" >> moves; echo "b5c6 d7c6" >> moves; echo draw >> moves
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     gnuan
wait    8
send    moves\r
wait    5
send    2\r
wait    4
send    1\r
wait    60

shot    gnuchess
size    24 80
cap     GNU Chess after 1. e4, answered with c7c5 out of its opening book,
cap     with a clock for each side and the search depth on the right.  It asks
cap     for a time control first (`1' is a move a minute) and takes moves as
cap     `e2e4'.  It reads TERMCAP as the terminal description itself, so source
cap     SYS/termcap.entry into your shell first.
try     gnuchess
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     gnuchess
wait    12
send    1\r
wait    10
send    e2e4\r
wait    22
snap
kill

shot    gnuchessc
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 built to be driven by `chesstool', which draws the board
cap     itself, so this one prints none: it takes a move as `e2e4' and prints
cap     its own reply on the next line.  For a board, see `gnuchess' or
cap     `nchess'.
try     gnuchessc
run     gnuchessc
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    12

shot    gnuchessn
cap     The squares drawn as blocks of hashes, so the light and dark ones can
cap     be told apart on a terminal with no highlighting.  It asks for a time
cap     control first (`1' is a move a minute) and takes moves as `e2e4'.  It
cap     reads TERMCAP as the terminal description itself, so source
cap     SYS/termcap.entry into your shell first.
try     gnuchessn
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     gnuchessn
wait    14
send    1\r
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    20
snap
kill

shot    gnuchessr
cap     The plainest GNU Chess display: pieces as letters, capitals for one
cap     side and lower case for the other.  It asks for a time control as
cap     `#moves #minutes' (40 moves in 5 minutes here) and for the operator
cap     time, then takes moves as `e2e4' and prints its reply.
try     gnuchessr
run     gnuchessr
wait    10
send    40 5\r
wait    4
send    0\r
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    20

shot    mille
cap     Mille Bornes, the French road-race card game: your hand on the left
cap     numbered 1 to 6, the deck and the discard beside it, the scoring
cap     table on the right and the commands under it -- `p' picks, `u #'
cap     plays a card, `d #' discards, `s' saves the game and `q' quits.
try     mille
run     mille
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    napoleon
size    24 80
cap     The opening: Napoleon states its licence, then describes the central
cap     office where the game begins -- the ebony desk, the chair you can
cap     twirl on, the one exit south -- and the scroll lying on the desk,
cap     which holds the licence in full.
try     napoleon
run     clear
wait    1
run     napoleon
wait    8
snap
send    quit\r
wait    3
send    q\r
wait    2

shot    nchess
size    36 80
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 with its plain display, thinking: asked for 40 moves in
cap     5 minutes, it shows the board in letters, then a line for each depth
cap     it searched with the score, the time, the nodes and the line it likes,
cap     and the totals at the foot before its reply.
try     nchess
run     nchess
wait    8
send    40 5\r
wait    4
send    0\r
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    25

shot    drawpoker
size    24 80
cap     Five-card draw against the computer.  Your hand is dealt face up at
cap     the top, the computer's face down below; stay, drop or bet, then
cap     name up to three cards to change.  F quits.
try     drawpoker
run     drawpoker
wait    5
send    \s
wait    3
keys    n
wait    5
snap
keys    F
wait    2
keys    y
wait    3

shot    poker
cap     Cold-hand poker against the computer: no rules asked for, a bet limit
cap     of 50 per cent of the pot and a 10-chip ante, then the deal -- five
cap     cards, and the choice to bet or check.  One of four games written in
cap     SNOBOL4; their data is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     poker
run     clear
wait    1
run     poker
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    50\r
wait    6
send    10\r
wait    8

shot    queens
size    34 80
cap     The eight-queens problem for any board: it reads the size as a number
cap     on standard input and draws every arrangement it finds with no two
cap     queens attacking.  A six-by-six board here, which has four.
try     echo 6 | queens
os9     echo 6 ! queens
run     clear
wait    1
run     echo 6 | queens
wait    8

shot    tttt
cap     Tic-tac-toe on a four-by-four board, two moves in.  Name a square by
cap     its column letter and row digit -- `b1' -- and the machine answers.
try     tttt
run     tttt
wait    4
send    b1\r
wait    3
send    c2\r
wait    5
snap
send    q
wait    2

# --------------------------------------------------------- chess utilities

shot    bincheckr
size    34 80
cap     Checks a GNU Chess binary opening book for how well it is packed:
cap     entry size, book size, pocket and count.  It reads a binary book
cap     layout older than anything on this disk -- GAMES/gnuchess.book is the
cap     text library -- so given that file it reports the entry size and
cap     zeroes, and then stops on a math trap as it averages over no entries:
cap     the register dump is the system reporting the trap.
try     bincheckr gnuchess.book
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     bincheckr gnuchess.book
wait    10

shot    checkgame
size    30 80
cap     A two-move game listed by gnuchessc's `list' command, replayed and
cap     checked move by move: the moves it accepted, and the board it
cap     reaches.  It stops one move short of the end of the list.
try     checkgame chess.lst
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G4CHK > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G4CHK
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G4CHK
run     echo e2e4 > moves; echo d2d4 >> moves; echo list >> moves; echo quit >> moves
wait    2
run     gnuchessc < moves > /nil 2>/nil
wait    25
run     cat CLp* > chess.lst
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat chess.lst
wait    4
run     checkgame chess.lst
wait    10

shot    game
size    24 80
cap     A two-move game, listed by gnuchessc's `list' command, drawn as
cap     PostScript: for each move, its number and score and then the board
cap     as a line of ChessFont characters, one rank to a `;'.  Put the
cap     ChessFont file from SRC/gnuchess/GNUCHESS4.0/MISC in front of it
cap     and send it to a PostScript printer.  Like checkgame it stops one
cap     move short of the end of the list.
try     game chess.lst
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G4GAME > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G4GAME
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G4GAME
run     echo e2e4 > moves; echo d2d4 >> moves; echo list >> moves; echo quit >> moves
wait    2
run     gnuchessc < moves > /nil 2>/nil
wait    25
run     cat CLp* > chess.lst
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     game chess.lst
wait    10

shot    postprint
size    24 80
cap     The positions GNU Chess has searched and kept in its hash file,
cap     printed as PostScript: for each, the best move, the search depth and
cap     the score, then the board as a line of ChessFont characters, one
cap     rank to a `;'.  The hash file shipped is empty; a short game with
cap     gnuchessc, out of its opening book, filled it first.  The file's
cap     name is built in, GNUCHESS4.0/MISC on /h0.
try     postprint | head -n 16
os9     postprint ! head -n 16
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G4PP > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G4PP
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G4PP
run     cat /h0/GNUCHESS4.0/MISC/gnuchess.hash > hash.save
wait    4
run     echo a2a3 > moves; echo h2h3 >> moves; echo quit >> moves
wait    2
run     gnuchessc < moves > /nil 2>/nil
wait    60
run     clear
wait    1
run     postprint | head -n 16
wait    12
snap
run     cat hash.save > /h0/GNUCHESS4.0/MISC/gnuchess.hash
wait    4

# ------------------------------------------------------------ dungeon crawl

shot    hackwish
size    24 80
cap     The end of a run: hack's goodbye as the game is saved, and the number
cap     of the game in which a new wizard started with a wand of wishing.
cap     hack's own screens pass unseen between.  With no argument it wishes
cap     for three potions of gain level; `hack' then carries on with the wish
cap     granted.
try     hackwish
run     clear
wait    1
run     hackwish
wait    75
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    hack
cap     Hack, the dungeon crawl NetHack grew out of: a room drawn in line
cap     characters, `@' for you, a letter for each monster -- `d' is a dog --
cap     and your hit points, armour class, strength and experience along the
cap     foot.  It relaunches itself by the name it was started with, so at
cap     bash give the whole pathlist shown; at the OS-9 shell plain `hack' is
cap     enough.  Its saves and scores live in GAMES/HACK/PLAYGROUND.
try     /dd/CMDS/GAMES/hack
os9     hack
run     /dd/CMDS/GAMES/hack
wait    10
send    n
wait    4
send    \s
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    nethack3
size    24 80
cap     A new character on the first level of the Mazes of Menace: the room,
cap     the `@' that is you and a pet beside you, and the two status lines --
cap     strength, dexterity and the rest, then dungeon level, gold, hit points,
cap     power, armour class and experience.  `y' at the first question lets it
cap     choose your character; `?' lists the commands, `S' saves, `Q' quits.
try     nethack3
run     export TERM=vt100
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     nethack3
wait    10
send    y
wait    8
send    \s
wait    4
send    \s
wait    4
snap
send    Q
wait    3
send    y
wait    4
send    n
wait    6

shot    moria
size    24 80
cap     A new warrior in the town at the top of the dungeon: the walls of the
cap     six shops, numbered by their doors, the `@' that is you, and down the
cap     left the character's stats, hit points and gold.  `?' lists the
cap     commands, `^X' saves and `^K' quits.
try     moria
run     export TERM=vt100 USER=tester
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     moria
wait    12
send    \s
wait    5
send    a
wait    4
send    m
wait    4
send    \e
wait    4
send    a
wait    5
send    Tester\r
wait    8
send    \s
wait    10
snap
send    \013
wait    4
send    y
wait    8


shot    trek
size    34 80
cap     A short game at novice skill, and the short-range scan of the first
cap     quadrant: the Enterprise is E, a starbase #, stars *, and beside the
cap     grid the ship's stardate, condition, energy, torpedoes and shields.
cap     `help' lists the commands; `terminate' ends the game.
try     trek
run     export TERM=vt100
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     trek
wait    4
send    \r
wait    2
send    s\r
wait    2
send    n\r
wait    2
send    x\r
wait    4
send    s\r
wait    4
snap
send    terminate\r
wait    2
send    n\r
wait    3

shot    atc
size    24 80
cap     The easy airport a few seconds in: the radar with its exits round the
cap     edge, airport 0 and a beacon, and the first plane as its letter and
cap     altitude.  Beside it the clock, the count of planes brought in safely,
cap     and each plane with where it is bound.  Talk to a plane by its letter;
cap     `?' lists what may come next, ^C quits.
try     atc -g easy
run     export TERM=vt100 USER=tester
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     atc -g easy
wait    14
snap
send    \003
wait    3
send    y
wait    6

shot    bandit
size    24 80
cap     A pull of the one-armed bandit: the three reels after the spin, the
cap     payoff table beside them, and the new total and the bet prompt below.
cap     `q' at the bet prompt walks away with the total.  It reads TERMCAP as
cap     the terminal description itself, so source SYS/termcap.entry into your
cap     shell first.
try     bandit
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     bandit
wait    5
send    n\r
wait    4
send    5\r
wait    5
snap
send    q\r
wait    4

shot    typefast
size    24 80
cap     A few seconds into an easy game: the words drift down toward the
cap     bottom border, and the hits and misses so far sit at the top.  Type a
cap     word and SPACE before it lands.  It reads TERMCAP as the terminal
cap     description itself, so source SYS/termcap.entry into your shell first.
try     typefast
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     typefast
wait    5
send    n
wait    3
send    1
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    castle
size    24 80
cap     The Realm of the Wizard: the corridor ahead is drawn in character
cap     graphics, with the character's stats down the left.  A few steps in,
cap     `c' and `a' cast the spell that tells you where you are.
try     castle
run     castle
wait    8
send    l
wait    3
send    k
wait    3
send    c
wait    3
send    a
wait    4
snap
send    \r
wait    2
send    q
wait    2
send    y
wait    4
send    \r
wait    3

shot    arithmetic
size    24 80
cap     Multiplication with operands up to 5: each problem waits for an
cap     answer and RETURN, a wrong one gets `What?' and the same problem
cap     again, and ^C ends the drill with the score so far.
try     arithmetic -o x -r 5
run     arithmetic -o x -r 5
wait    3
send    12\r
wait    2
send    6\r
wait    2
send    \003
wait    3
snap

shot    sod
size    24 80
cap     A few steps into the easiest level: each square walked on shows how
cap     many of its neighbours would sink you, and the X at the bottom right
cap     is the way out.
try     sod -l1
run     sod -l1
wait    5
send    l
wait    2
send    l
wait    2
send    j
wait    2
send    l
wait    3
snap
send    q
wait    3
send    \r
wait    3

shot    jumble
size    28 80
cap     All twenty-four orderings of the letters of `team', one to a line.
cap     Reading down the list for real words finds meat, mate and tame.
try     jumble team
run     jumble team
wait    2
snap

shot    rogue
size    24 80
cap     The first level of the dungeon: the room is drawn as it is found, `@'
cap     is you, and the line at the foot keeps your level, gold, hit points,
cap     strength, armour and experience.
try     rogue
run     rogue
wait    6
snap
send    Q
wait    3
send    y
wait    4
send    \s
wait    3
send    \s
wait    3

shot    larn
cap     The first level of the dungeon, drawn as you find it with letters for
cap     what is in it; the two lines at the foot are your spells, armour
cap     class, level and the six attributes.  RETURN gets past the opening
cap     text.
try     larn
run     larn
wait    12
send    \r
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    ularn
cap     ULarn: it asks you to pick a character class -- ogre, wizard, klingon,
cap     elf, rogue, geek, dwarf or rambo -- and drops you in the dungeon, here
cap     as a wizard, with your spells, armour class and attributes along the
cap     foot.  Its fortunes, help, maps and scores are in GAMES/ULARN.
try     ularn
run     ularn
wait    10
keys    b
wait    5
send    \r
wait    5
snap
send    \003
wait    3

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ puzzles

shot    maze
cap     A maze generator small enough to have won an obfuscated-C contest.  It
cap     reads the number of rows on standard input and draws a maze that many
cap     rows tall, as wide as the terminal.
try     echo 11 | maze
os9     echo 11 ! maze
run     echo 11 | maze
wait    8

shot    mines
cap     Minesweeper on a sixteen-by-sixteen board with forty mines.  Name a
cap     square by its row letter and then its column letter; answer the
cap     `Mark?' prompt with Y to flag it instead of opening it.  Opening a
cap     square clears everything around it that is safe and numbers the
cap     edges with how many mines they touch.  `q' quits.
try     mines
run     mines
wait    8
send    H\r
wait    3
send    H\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    5
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    animal
cap     Four yes answers down the tree in DOC/animal's example file -- living,
cap     human, female, a politician -- and its final guess.  When a guess is
cap     wrong it asks what you were thinking of and a question that tells the
cap     two apart, and writes them into the file.
try     animal example
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC/animal
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     animal example
wait    6
send    y\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    4
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    bog
cap     A tray of sixteen letters with 2:54 left on the three-minute clock:
cap     type every word you can trace through adjoining letters.  `?' shows
cap     the rules.
try     bog
run     bog
wait    8
send    \s
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    hang
cap     Six letters guessed, a, e, i, o, r and t: the seven-letter word shows
cap     the t, the a and the i in place, and the guessed letters are gone from
cap     the list at the top.  Type a letter to guess it.
try     hang
run     hang
wait    6
keys    aeiort
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    2

# -------------------------------------------------------------- other games

shot    fuddle
size    24 80
cap     Chess against the computer, written for a TeleVideo terminal: it
cap     clears the screen and moves the cursor with TeleVideo codes, which an
cap     ANSI terminal like this one does not follow, so the pieces and the
cap     board land in the wrong places.  The moves still read: at the top
cap     right, after `fuddling...', its word for thinking, it answers e2e4
cap     with d7 to d5.
try     fuddle
run     fuddle
wait    6
send    y\r
wait    14
send    e2e4\r
wait    20
snap

shot    hotel
cap     hotel, a hotel-chain board game in the manner of Acquire, played
cap     against the computer: it asks how many are playing -- two here --
cap     seats you among them at random and plays the others itself.  It
cap     draws the board, the numbered tiles in hand and the seven chains'
cap     shares, and each turn you play a tile by its number.
try     hotel
run     hotel
wait    5
send    N\r
wait    4
send    2\r
wait    5
send    Alice\r
wait    4
send    Bob\r
wait    8

shot    stone
cap     A stones game in the Nim family, set up by four answers that it does
cap     not echo: no rules, a pile of 21, winner's parity 1 and at most 3
cap     taken at a time.  Now it asks for your first take.  One of four games
cap     written in SNOBOL4; their data is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     stone
run     stone
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    21\r
wait    6
send    1\r
wait    6
send    3\r
wait    8

shot    draw
for     draw
cap     A character-graphics drawing program: a box drawn on its canvas by
cap     dropping the pen with `p' and walking it round with the l, j, h and k
cap     keys, then a diagonal in `*' with the pen character changed by
cap     `\*' and the n key.  `?' lists the rest of the keys; ESC leaves.
try     draw
run     draw
wait    8
keys    p
keys    llllllllllllllllllll
keys    jjjjjjjj
keys    hhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhh
keys    kkkkkkkk
keys    \*
keys    nnnnnnnn
wait    3
snap
send    \033
wait    3

shot    lorenz3d
for     lorenz3d
cap     The Lorenz attractor in three dimensions, asked for parameter by
cap     parameter: the time step, the z magnification and offset, then
cap     Lorenz's own a, b and c -- 10 and 28 here, with 8/3 for c the value
cap     that makes the butterfly.  It then draws in Tektronix vector codes,
cap     for a Tektronix graphics terminal.
try     lorenz3d
run     lorenz3d
wait    4
send    0.01\r
wait    2
send    20\r
wait    2
send    0\r
wait    2
send    10\r
wait    2
send    28\r
wait    3
snap
send    2.667\r
wait    2
kill


# The rest of "Other games", one stanza each.

shot    tt
cap     Tetris for terminals, a few pieces in: , and / move a piece, .
cap     rotates it, space drops it, s pauses and q quits.
try     tt
run     tt
wait    4
keys    ,,.
wait    2
send    \s
wait    3
keys    //
wait    2
send    \s
wait    3
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    zot
size    44 80
cap     Prints a line of text in one of fourteen animated styles: the
cap     letters slide in from a side, bounce, sort themselves, or turn up
cap     one at a time like a game-show board, and settle as the line.  Shown
cap     as a strip, one line per frame, top to bottom: Wheel of Fortune
cap     (style 14), then Expand text outward (style 5).  zot -s names all
cap     fourteen.
try     zot -s=14 "Microware OS-9/68000 -- the freeware collection, 1987 to 1996"
frames
run     zot -s=14 "Microware OS-9/68000 -- the freeware collection, 1987 to 1996"
wait    6
run     zot -s=5 "Microware OS-9/68000 -- the freeware collection, 1987 to 1996"
wait    6

shot    convert
cap     Starts the WORLD text adventure.  Its opening sets you on a trail near
cap     the top of a pass on a strange world, your landing craft and the dome
cap     that houses the rest of your party far below, and leaves you at the
cap     `>' prompt.
try     convert
run     convert
wait    8
snap
send    quit\r
wait    3

shot    vtxtcn
cap     A build step for the World adventure: it packs the game's text from
cap     vtext.dat, in the current directory, into q1text.dat and writes the
cap     qtext, objdes and gtext tables as .inc files for the compile.  It
cap     reports the packed and unpacked sizes.  vtext.dat ships in SRC/world.
try     vtxtcn
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/VTXT > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/VTXT; cp /dd/SRC/world/vtext.dat /dd/tmp/VTXT/vtext.dat
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/VTXT
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     vtxtcn
wait    20
run     ls -l
wait    4

shot    mkdict
cap     Builds bog's dictionary from a word list: it reads the list on
cap     standard input, keeps the words bog can use, and writes them
cap     compressed, each sharing the start of the word before it.  Here a copy
cap     of GAMES/words, 164,034 bytes, becomes a dictionary of 80,520, the
cap     size of bog's own in GAMES/BOG, which bog reads through the index
cap     mkindex makes.
try     mkdict < words > dict
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G4BOG > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G4BOG
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G4BOG
run     cat /dd/GAMES/words > words
wait    6
run     clear
wait    1
run     mkdict < words > dict
wait    25
run     ls -l
wait    4

shot    mkindex
size    32 80
cap     The index bog reads its dictionary through, made from a dictionary
cap     on standard input -- here a copy of the one in GAMES/BOG: a line
cap     for each first letter, with the offsets where its words start and
cap     end.  bog's own is that output kept as dict.ind beside the
cap     dictionary.
try     mkindex < dict
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G4BOGI > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G4BOGI
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G4BOGI
run     cat /dd/GAMES/BOG/dict > dict
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     mkindex < dict
wait    15

shot    wisecrack
cap     A server: started in the background with the `&', it feeds slogans
cap     from a German OS-9 club seminar of 1992-93 into /pipe/txtpipe and
cap     prints nothing itself.  Each `ask' then pulls out the next one --
cap     "VIEL SPASS MIT OSK" is "have fun with OSK".  Stop it when you have
cap     finished with `signal <id> 0', the id bash printed in angle brackets.
try     wisecrack &
run     wisecrack &
wait    3
run     ask
wait    5
run     ask
wait    5
run     signal 4 0
wait    3

shot    ask
cap     Two calls, two slogans, in German, pulled from the pipe wisecrack
cap     writes to: they come from a 1992-93 OS-9 club seminar.  Start the
cap     server first with `wisecrack &'; on its own ask says "No Wisecracks
cap     coming".  When you have finished, stop the server with `signal <id> 0',
cap     the id bash printed.
try     ask
run     wisecrack &
wait    3
run     ask
wait    5
run     ask
wait    5
run     signal 4 0
wait    3

shot    shuffle
cap     A switch puzzle.  A row of numbered switches is wired together so
cap     that flipping one flips the switches linked to it; you choose a
cap     switch by number at the `Wich switch ?' prompt, and the aim is to
cap     line them all up.  The level and your move count are along the foot;
cap     `q' quits.
try     shuffle
run     shuffle
wait    6
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    cyberwar
cap     A game written for G-Windows: at a terminal it exits without a word,
cap     with status 221. It needs G-Windows.
try     cyberwar; echo "status $?"
run     cyberwar; echo "status $?"
wait    6

shot    puzzle
cap     A sliding-tile puzzle for G-Windows, the graphics system this disk
cap     does not carry, so at a terminal it cannot draw and stops.  It is
cap     here for a real OS-9 workstation that has the display.
try     puzzle
run     puzzle
wait    6

shot    scriptmaster
cap     A scripting tool for G-Windows: at a terminal it exits at once with
cap     status 208, an unknown service request -- the G-Windows calls it makes
cap     are not there.  It needs G-Windows.
try     scriptmaster; echo "status $?"
run     scriptmaster; echo "status $?"
wait    5

shot    colortest
cap     At a terminal: it looks for /win, the G-Windows display, and stops
cap     because there is none.  It needs G-Windows to report on.
